ERROR REPORTS

Please send errors and suggestions to Frans Kaashoek and Robert Morris
(kaashoek,rtm@mit.edu).  The main purpose of xv6 is as a teaching
operating system for MIT's 6.1810, so we are more interested in
simplifications and clarifications than new features.

BUILDING AND RUNNING XV6

You will need a RISC-V "newlib" tool chain from
https://github.com/riscv/riscv-gnu-toolchain, and qemu compiled for
riscv64-softmmu.  Once they are installed, and in your shell
search path, you can run "make qemu".

Functionalities:
1)Crash/Error Reporter — when a kernel error occurs, save/display the error context.
2)System Statistics Counter — count interrupts, system calls, context switches, allocations, etc.
3)Kernel Alarm/Reminder — schedule a kernel event to happen after N seconds.
4)Kernel Theme/Color Module — different terminal themes/modes for your kernel shell.
5)Kernel Mini-Games Module — tiny games such as Snake, Tic-Tac-Toe, or Guess the Number running directly through the kernel shell.
6)Process Search Command — findproc chrome / findproc 03-style searching through your process table.
7)CPU Usage History — instead of showing only current CPU usage, keep the last 30 measurements and display a tiny graph.
8)Resource Warning System — automatically warn when memory/process count/etc. crosses a threshold.
9)Kernel Benchmark Mode — run simple CPU/memory tests and report execution time.
10)System Information Command — your own sysinfo showing kernel version, architecture, memory, CPU information, uptime, and build date.
